# token-review

A task that costs too much is rarely a bad Crew. It is usually a project that does not tell a Crew what it needs: where things are, how to build and test, what was already decided.
Run this only when the captain asks; it spends your own context.

You read two commands and nothing else: `{{.MatevBin}} usage {{.ProjectName}} --top 5` and `{{.MatevBin}} usage {{.ProjectName}} <crew> --why`.
Never open a transcript or the repository to explain a cost. When `--why` cannot answer a question that matters, propose a scout with that question to the captain.

## Pick at most three tasks

"Too many" is a comparison, never a number on its own.
Compare a task by `TASK-TOTAL` (the task plus its reviewers) with tasks of the same kind, size, model and effort: a scout with scouts, a one-file fix with one-file fixes.
A task is worth explaining when it is several times its peers, when its context neared the model's window, or when the captain names it. With no peer on file, say so.
Your own row is the price of coordination: give its share, and leave its causes to the captain's dashboard.

## Read why

`--why` prints one bounded page. Hold to its limits: findings overlap, so never add them up or call one the cost of a command; `?` is unknown, not zero; nothing under `not measured` supports a conclusion; `Unclassified` and `Mixed` are not causes.

## Name the cause and its owner

Quote the line that shows it, and match one row:

| The page shows | Likely cause | Where the fix lives |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Large `Research / inspect`; `repeated_read` | The Crew had to discover the layout | A repo map in the repo's `AGENTS.md`; the brief's `## What we already know` |
| Large `Read instructions`; large instruction files loaded | Too much loaded on every call | Trim the repo's `AGENTS.md`; move procedures into repo skills |
| `execution_failure`, `repeated_error`, retest loops | Build or test steps undocumented, wrong or flaky | A build-and-test part of the repo's `AGENTS.md`, or a repo skill; a flaky test is its own task |
| `large_output` | Commands dump whole logs | Quiet or targeted commands documented in the repo |
| `process_polling`, a large `Wait / poll` share | Slow jobs polled by the model | The fast path documented; a brief that says wait once |
| High peak context, many calls, a fix round costlier than the first | The task was too large for one session | How you split work; a tighter `Out of scope:` |
| A costliest prompt is your correction after a hand-back; questions asked | The brief left the Crew to guess, or acceptance was not checkable | The brief (`new-task`), `## Captain's decisions`, a line for `{{.ProjectCrewDoc}}` |
| Large output on a simple task, on a heavy profile | The profile was heavier than the work | The dispatch table's rules |

When nothing matches, say the record does not explain the cost and name what is not measured. Never invent a cause.
A standing change needs a pattern: the same cause in two tasks, or once with a mechanical reason every Crew will hit. One bad afternoon is a note, not a rule; a rule costs every later Crew the tokens to read it.

## Report

Write the review to `{{.MateDir}}/token-reviews/<YYYY-MM-DD>.md`:

```markdown
# Token review <YYYY-MM-DD>

## Tasks
### <crew> - <total>, <calls> calls, peak context <n>
- Compared with: <peers and their totals, or "no peer on file">
- Cause: <one row of the table>
- Evidence: `<the line of --why that shows it, verbatim>`
- Confidence: pattern | single case | not explained

## Proposals
### <n>. <what changes>
- Destination: <repo>:`AGENTS.md` | <repo>:`.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` | <repo>:`docs/<file>` | `{{.ProjectCrewDoc}}` | `{{.ProjectDoc}}` | the dispatch table | how I brief or split
- Text: <the exact lines to add, or the skill's name, trigger and outline>
- From: <the tasks and evidence it rests on>
- Check: <which number on the next comparable task should move, and which way>
```

What you cannot write without knowing the code (the real test command, the real directory map) is not guessed: the proposal says a Crew fills it in.
Then tell the captain in a few lines: which tasks, the causes in outcome words, the proposals by number, and the file.

## Apply and check

- A change inside a repo is a ship Crew's commit, yours to dispatch, with the captain's request as `## Captain's words` and the proposal under `## Build`.
- A line for `{{.ProjectCrewDoc}}` or a dispatch rule is the captain's to add: give them the exact line.
- A PROJECT.md fact or a lesson about how you brief or split is yours, as `stow` says, sourced to the review file.

A proposal is a prediction. When the next comparable task closes, run `--why` on it and add one line under the proposal: moved, did not move, or not comparable.
Never report a saving you did not measure. A proposal that did not move its number is withdrawn or rewritten, and the captain is told.
